 title:'The smart campus',focus:'Distinguish evidence from assumptions; build an argument with concessions.',
 readingTitle:'When a campus becomes a living laboratory',
 paragraphs:[
 'A. In 2018, Northbridge University announced that it would use its campus as a living laboratory for energy research. Rather than constructing a separate demonstration building, it fitted sensors in existing lecture theatres and residences. The sensors recorded room temperature and electricity use, but did not identify individual occupants. Researchers hoped that everyday buildings would provide more realistic information than a specially designed laboratory. Students could also use anonymised data in coursework. The project therefore had two aims: reduce energy consumption and make research part of ordinary university life.',
 'B. The first results appeared impressive. Electricity use in three monitored buildings fell by 18 per cent during the first year. However, the buildings had also been closed for six weeks for repairs, and that winter was unusually mild. A later analysis compared energy use only on occupied days and adjusted for outside temperature. Under this method, the estimated reduction was 7 per cent. The revised figure did not mean the project had failed. It showed how a promising headline could conceal several explanations and why the choice of comparison mattered.',
 'C. The technical system was not the only influence on behaviour. Initially, a dashboard displayed the campus total as one large number. Students said that they could not connect this number with their own routines. Designers replaced it with weekly building-level comparisons and practical suggestions. Residents began reporting heating faults and switching off equipment in shared rooms. Yet the designers avoided public league tables between residences: they feared these would reward buildings with fewer occupants rather than those whose occupants made greater efforts.',
 'D. Participation remained uneven. Engineering students were heavily represented in the first research projects, while arts students rarely joined them. The university responded by offering short workshops requiring no programming experience. In one workshop, students designed posters explaining uncertainty in the energy figures. In another, they interviewed residents about comfort. The organisers argued that a living laboratory needed people who could interpret social behaviour as well as people who could maintain equipment. They did not claim that every student needed to become a researcher.',
 'E. Expansion created a financial question. Sensors were relatively inexpensive to buy, but someone had to calibrate them, replace damaged units and check the data. A grant covered these duties for the first two years. Thereafter, the university assigned a permanent maintenance budget. The project leader warned that an institution could easily install visible technology while neglecting the less visible work that kept it useful. No estimate of the total maintenance cost was released in the project report.',
 'F. Northbridge concluded that the laboratory had value, but rejected a single measure of success. Energy savings, student participation and the quality of research all mattered. Some outcomes would take years to assess; students might apply the methods in jobs long after graduation. Other universities were invited to adapt the model rather than copy its equipment list. The central lesson was that useful innovation required reliable comparison, ongoing maintenance and opportunities for people with different skills to participate.'
 ],
