 {title:'Part B · Interpreting energy results',script:'Today I want to explain why a lower energy bill does not automatically prove that an intervention worked. Imagine that we install sensors and see consumption fall by eighteen per cent. That sounds convincing, but we must consider what else changed. Perhaps buildings were closed, or the weather was warmer. We therefore compare occupied days and adjust for temperature. In our case, this reduced the estimated saving to seven per cent. That is still useful, but it is a more cautious conclusion. We also interview residents because numbers cannot explain every behaviour. Some residents reported faults after seeing weekly comparisons. Finally, maintenance matters. A cheap sensor can become expensive if nobody checks its accuracy. Our next step is to compare similar buildings without sensors, although assigning buildings randomly is not practical at this stage.',questions:[
 choice('l7','What is the lecturer’s main warning?',['Energy bills are always inaccurate.','A fall in consumption may have several causes.','Weather should never be considered.'],1,'The lecturer distinguishes observed change from proof of an intervention’s effect.'),gap('l8','ONE WORD: The analysis adjusts for outside ___.',['temperature'],'Occupied days and temperature are the two adjustments.'),gap('l9','ONE WORD: Researchers also ___ residents.',['interview'],'Interviews help explain behaviour that numbers alone cannot explain.'),choice('l10','What did some residents do after seeing comparisons?',['Reported faults','Bought sensors','Moved buildings'],0,'Reporting faults is the behaviour explicitly described.'),gap('l11','ONE WORD: Someone must check a sensor’s ___.','accuracy','The lecturer links maintenance cost with checking accuracy.'),choice('l12','What is the proposed next step?',['Randomly assign every resident','Compare similar buildings without sensors','Stop collecting information'],1,'Random assignment is described as impractical; similar unmonitored buildings are proposed.') ]}
